WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.969
Every night, the payments company pays each shop what it took that day.

2
00:00:06.969 --> 00:00:12.441
One job builds the payout file, the list that tells the bank how much to send to each shop.

3
00:00:12.733 --> 00:00:14.405
It must run exactly once.

4
00:00:14.405 --> 00:00:17.281
Run it twice, and every shop is paid twice.

5
00:00:17.281 --> 00:00:23.100
The job runs on three servers, so that one dying on the night doesn't stop the payouts.

6
00:00:23.367 --> 00:00:25.284
So, the tempting fix: a lock.

7
00:00:25.284 --> 00:00:30.839
Before it starts, a server takes a lock that only one may hold, and the others wait.

8
00:00:30.839 --> 00:00:35.269
We keep it in Redis, a fast store that all three servers can reach.

9
00:00:35.533 --> 00:00:39.100
Inside one program, nobody can take a lock away from you.

10
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:45.357
But a lock shared by three servers has to run out, or a dead holder would block the payouts forever.

11
00:00:45.357 --> 00:00:48.673
So this one is a lease, with a deadline: ten seconds.

12
00:00:48.967 --> 00:00:50.231
Watch one night.

13
00:00:50.231 --> 00:00:52.127
Server A takes the lock.

14
00:00:52.127 --> 00:00:54.576
Server B tries, and is refused.

15
00:00:54.833 --> 00:00:59.521
Then A's program freezes, for fifteen seconds, while it cleans up its memory.

16
00:00:59.521 --> 00:01:03.721
Every part of it stops, including the part that would renew the lock.

17
00:01:03.967 --> 00:01:08.521
After ten seconds, Redis deletes the lock, exactly as asked.

18
00:01:08.521 --> 00:01:09.356
B takes it.

19
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.188
B builds the payout file, and writes it.

20
00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:13.533
Then A wakes up.

21
00:01:13.533 --> 00:01:20.267
It carries on from where it stopped, and writes its file: built before B's run, and missing 12 shops.

22
00:01:20.533 --> 00:01:22.187
From A's side, it blinked.

23
00:01:22.187 --> 00:01:24.540
And no check inside A can catch this.

24
00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:27.593
We saw that the freeze can land after any check.

25
00:01:27.867 --> 00:01:32.232
So if A can't know it's late, someone else has to refuse its write.

26
00:01:32.232 --> 00:01:33.600
Who sees every write?

27
00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:36.207
Only the thing being written: the store.

28
00:01:36.467 --> 00:01:39.414
Picture the deli counter at a supermarket.

29
00:01:39.414 --> 00:01:40.958
You pull a ticket: 33.

30
00:01:40.958 --> 00:01:42.712
The next person pulls 34.

31
00:01:42.712 --> 00:01:51.555
Once the counter has served 34, someone who turns up late holding 33 is sent away, however sure they are that it's their turn.

32
00:01:51.767 --> 00:01:52.230
Map it.

33
00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:56.403
The ticket is a number the lock hands out with each new holder.

34
00:01:56.403 --> 00:01:58.059
The counter is the store.

35
00:01:58.333 --> 00:02:01.231
Let's play the night again, with numbers.

36
00:02:01.231 --> 00:02:03.492
Next to the lock sits a counter.

37
00:02:03.492 --> 00:02:04.269
It's at 32.

38
00:02:04.269 --> 00:02:08.792
The store remembers the highest number it has accepted: also 32.

39
00:02:09.033 --> 00:02:14.432
Server A takes the lock, and in the same step the counter goes up by one.

40
00:02:14.432 --> 00:02:15.763
It holds token 33.

41
00:02:15.763 --> 00:02:19.165
B's attempt is refused, and uses up no number.

42
00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:20.696
Server A freezes.

43
00:02:20.696 --> 00:02:25.728
Ten seconds later the lock is gone, and B takes it, with token 34.

44
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:28.602
B writes its file with fence 34.

45
00:02:28.602 --> 00:02:30.146
The store compares.

46
00:02:30.146 --> 00:02:35.919
34 is at least the 32 it has seen, so it accepts, and now remembers 34.

47
00:02:36.167 --> 00:02:39.785
Now A wakes up and writes its file, with fence 33.

48
00:02:39.785 --> 00:02:41.450
What does the store do?

49
00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:42.608
Pause and guess.

50
00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:46.897
It refuses.

51
00:02:46.897 --> 00:02:50.788
33 is below the 34 it already accepted.

52
00:02:50.788 --> 00:02:52.284
B's file stays.

53
00:02:52.533 --> 00:02:54.863
However sure A is that it's its turn.

54
00:02:54.863 --> 00:03:00.845
That number is called a fencing token: it fences out every holder older than the newest writer.

55
00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:03.120
Here the deli picture breaks.

56
00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:08.065
The deli calls each number itself, so it knows when 34 is being served.

57
00:03:08.065 --> 00:03:11.199
The store learns about 34 only when B writes.

58
00:03:11.199 --> 00:03:15.377
Had A woken up before that, its 33 would have been accepted.

59
00:03:15.633 --> 00:03:16.697
And that's fine.

60
00:03:16.697 --> 00:03:23.412
Fencing only refuses a holder once a newer one has written, which is exactly the case that does harm.

61
00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:24.751
How is it built?

62
00:03:24.751 --> 00:03:31.189
In Redis, the counter is one command, increment, run in the same small script that takes the lock.

63
00:03:31.189 --> 00:03:34.408
So a number is handed out exactly when a lock is.

64
00:03:34.667 --> 00:03:39.604
The store's side is the half people miss, because it isn't lock code at all.

65
00:03:39.604 --> 00:03:47.400
In a database table, the write gets one extra condition: change the row only if its stored fence is no larger than mine.

66
00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:50.388
Zero rows changed means: you're a late holder.

67
00:03:50.633 --> 00:03:51.826
Now the fine print.

68
00:03:51.826 --> 00:03:59.927
The counter must never go backwards, so it gets no deadline, and it must live where it can't be thrown away when memory fills up.

69
00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:02.934
And it's only as safe as that one Redis server.

70
00:04:02.934 --> 00:04:10.496
If the server dies before its last increments were copied to its spare, a new holder can get a number that was already handed out.

71
00:04:10.767 --> 00:04:21.483
That's why the numbers are safer in etcd or ZooKeeper, two systems built so a group of servers agrees on every change, and both already keep a number like this.

72
00:04:21.767 --> 00:04:24.089
And ask one question about any lock.

73
00:04:24.089 --> 00:04:28.154
Is it for efficiency, so two servers don't waste the same work?

74
00:04:28.154 --> 00:04:29.897
A single Redis key will do.

75
00:04:29.897 --> 00:04:34.155
Or is it for correctness, where a double run pays the shops twice?

76
00:04:34.155 --> 00:04:36.413
Then the store itself has to check.

77
00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:37.628
So, the moral.

78
00:04:37.628 --> 00:04:41.539
A lock keeps holders apart only while every lease is alive.

79
00:04:41.539 --> 00:04:45.915
The token keeps them apart at the one place that sees every write.

80
00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:47.758
One quick check.

81
00:04:47.758 --> 00:04:49.964
The store has accepted 34.

82
00:04:49.964 --> 00:04:52.679
A server shows up with token 35.

83
00:04:52.679 --> 00:04:54.206
Accept, or refuse?

84
00:04:54.206 --> 00:04:55.564
Pause and think.

85
00:04:58.800 --> 00:04:59.340
Accept.

86
00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:02.814
35 is newer than anything the store has seen.

87
00:05:02.814 --> 00:05:06.905
It's the next holder, and the store now remembers 35.
